 # Decisions
 
+## 2026-06-01 — Snapshot tests are advisory (non-blocking) on `release/ios16-only` (PR #76)
+- **Decision:** Mark the "Snapshot tests (iPad simulator)" CI step `continue-on-error: true` in `.github/workflows/ios-build.yml`. The step still runs and logs visual diffs for human review, but a mismatch no longer fails the build.
+- **Why:** The baseline PNGs committed in `ios/DrumrotSnapshotTests/__Snapshots__/` (`9caa84b`, recorded on a developer's Mac) have never matched the CI runner's renderer — cross-machine font hinting / anti-aliasing differences. Result: the snapshot suite hard-failed on *every* CI run since it was added, blocking an otherwise-passing build (Build + Unit + UI all green). These diffs are not real regressions, so they must not gate the iOS-16 release branch whose job is shipping TestFlight builds. The workflow's own comments already described snapshots as a "soft failure" on first run, so advisory matches original intent.
+- **Alternatives rejected:**
+  - *Regenerate baselines on the CI runner and commit them* — keeps the suite gating/meaningful, but needs an artifact round-trip (delete local PNGs → CI records → download → commit) and ongoing "re-bless the images" friction on every intentional UI change. User explicitly chose against this for a release branch.
+  - *Delete the baselines so CI records fresh every run* — green, but the suite would never actually compare against anything (degenerate; guards nothing).
+- **User chose:** advisory (option #1), accepting that accidental visual regressions won't fail CI — they're only visible by reading the step's logged diffs.
+- **Consequences:** To restore gating later, record baselines *on the CI runner itself* and commit them, then flip the step back to hard-fail. Until then, snapshot diffs are an FYI in the CI log, not a gate. Intentional UI redesigns no longer require a baseline-update commit on this branch.
+- **Context — what unblocked this:** the snapshot mismatch only became visible after fixing a compile-error cascade (commits `deeff4b`, `e327eb8`, `05201fe`) where the `main`→`ios16` merge had leaked SwiftData test APIs (`AppModelContainer`/`.modelContainer`/`makeContext`/`PersistenceService`/`FetchDescriptor`) into this UserDefaults-based branch. Recurring hazard: any cherry-pick/merge from `main` that touches test targets must be re-checked for SwiftData-only symbols, since `release/ios16-only` dropped SwiftData entirely.
+
 ## 2026-05-27 — Adopt orchetect/MIDIKit (SwiftMIDI 1.x) for CoreMIDI input (#59 closes #57)
 - **Decision:** Replace the hand-rolled CoreMIDI plumbing in `Drumrot/MIDI/MIDIInputManager.swift` with a thin wrapper over orchetect's MIDIKit library — added via SPM as the umbrella package URL `https://github.com/orchetect/MIDIKit`, "Up to Next Major Version", linked product **`SwiftMIDI`** on the Drumrot target. MIDIKit 1.1.0 resolved (transitive pins for swift-midi-io 1.1.0, swift-midi-core 1.0.1, swift-midi-controlsurfaces, -file, -sync, -ui, -timecode, swift-data-parsing).
 - **Why:** Closes #57's silent-receive root cause on iOS 16 (Roland TD-50X USB devices enumerate but the UMP receive path stays silent). MIDIKit transparently falls back to the legacy `MIDIInputPortCreateWithBlock` path on iOS 16/17 while still surfacing MIDI 2.0 events internally — i.e. the exact dual-path fix #57 originally proposed hand-rolling, already battle-tested in the AudioKit ecosystem. Trade: one SPM dep (Apache-2.0, iOS 12+, pure-Swift, ~300–500 KB est.) for ~70 LOC of CoreMIDI compatibility shim we'd otherwise own + maintain.
